---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="ARM9 menu string pool"
  description="The 26 short action verbs / quantity labels embedded in the ARM9 binary's data section, plus the 20-string NPC interaction menu in ov096 and the equip/remake slots in ov170/171. All width-budgeted by fixed slot stride."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'Names & Words', href: `${base}/field-guide/names-and-words/` },
    { label: 'ARM9 menu pool' },
  ]}
>
  <h2>String pool location</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>File: <code>arm9_full_plaintext.bin</code> (BLZ-decompressed ARM9, 723 KB)</li>
    <li>Pool start: file offset <code>0xa6e44</code> → RAM <code>0x020a6e44</code></li>
    <li>Pool end:   file offset <code>0xa6f88</code> → RAM <code>0x020a6f88</code></li>
    <li>26 string records, total span: ~324 bytes (varies; record sizes differ)</li>
    <li>Strings are SHIFT-JIS-style packed bytes (game's internal short encoding)</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>"Plaintext-ARM9" trick</h2>
  <p>
    The original ROM ships ARM9 in BLZ-compressed form (564,488 bytes at ROM
    offset <code>0x4000</code>). Editing the embedded string pool in place
    is awkward because the strings live <em>after</em> the BLZ-decompressed
    boundary. Patching the Nintendo SDK module-params field
    <code>CompressedStaticEnd := StaticBSSStart</code> (where
    <code>StaticBSSStart = 0x020A87A0</code>) makes the boot ROM skip BLZ
    decompression entirely — the ARM9 is loaded as plaintext at boot.
    Decompressed size: 723,032 bytes (<code>0xB0858</code>). Validated in
    <code>notes/arm9_passthrough_test_RESULTS.md</code>. See
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/file-formats/blz-arm9/`}>BLZ &amp; the plaintext-ARM9 trick</a>
    for the full format breakdown.
  </p>

  <h2>Slot widths</h2>
  <p>
    The patcher fits each EN string into the slot's pre-allocated byte width.
    Slot column = byte budget. Strings that overflow even the canon shortened
    form are marked <strong>CO</strong> (canon-overflow) and will be restored
    if a pool relocator is added later.
  </p>

  <h2>ARM9 pool — 26 strings</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>#</th><th>file off</th><th>slot</th><th>EN</th><th>CO?</th><th>source</th><th>JP / G1 canon note</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {ARM9.map((r, i) => (
        <tr>
          <td>{i + 1}</td>
          <td><code>{r.off}</code></td>
          <td>{r.slot}</td>
          <td><strong>{r.en}</strong></td>
          <td>{r.co ? 'CO' : ''}</td>
          <td>{r.src}</td>
          <td><code>{r.jp}</code></td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Overlay single-string slots (ov047 / ov059 / ov065)</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Overlay</th><th>file off</th><th>slot</th><th>EN</th><th>source</th><th>JP / canon notes</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>ov047</td><td><code>0x1c70</code></td><td>20</td><td>Show BGM</td><td>fresh</td><td>ショーのおんがく</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ov059</td><td><code>0x1b2c</code></td><td>12</td><td>Magic</td><td>fresh</td><td>とんがり (G1 dropped "Pointy")</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ov065</td><td><code>0x3378</code></td><td>40</td><td>Like this? (Y/N)</td><td>fresh</td><td>これでいいかい？ (confirm prompt)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Overlay 096 — NPC interaction menu (20 strings)</h2>
  <p>
    The friend-chat / NPC interaction menu. All fresh translations except
    Mystery and Rumors which lean on Game 1 corpus parallels
    (<code>ふしぎ → mystery</code>, <code>ウワサ → rumor</code>) and Berry's
    which is the shop name carried verbatim from friend-chat dialog. The first
    slot (<code>&lt;&lt;&lt;</code>) is canon-overflow for "Back".
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>#</th><th>file off</th><th>slot</th><th>EN</th><th>source</th><th>JP</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {OV96.map((r, i) => (
        <tr>
          <td>{i + 1}</td>
          <td><code>{r.off}</code></td>
          <td>{r.slot}</td>
          <td><strong>{r.en}</strong></td>
          <td>{r.src}</td>
          <td><code>{r.jp}</code></td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Overlay 170 / 171 — equip + remake slots</h2>
  <p>
    Two late-game / extra-event overlays. The equip slots align with Game 1
    canon (<code>みにつける → Equip</code>); the remake slots ship as a fresh
    translation of <code>つくりなおす</code>, choosing "Remake" over the more
    literal "Redo" to preserve the dress-up meaning.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Overlay</th><th>file off</th><th>slot</th><th>EN</th><th>source</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>ov170</td><td><code>0x1d50</code></td><td>16</td><td><strong>Equip</strong></td><td>canon-G1 ✓</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ov171</td><td><code>0x740</code></td><td>12</td><td><strong>Equip</strong></td><td>canon-G1 ✓</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ov171</td><td><code>0x74c</code></td><td>12</td><td><strong>Equip</strong></td><td>canon-G1 ✓</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ov171</td><td><code>0x758</code></td><td>16</td><td><strong>Remake</strong></td><td>fresh</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ov171</td><td><code>0x768</code></td><td>16</td><td><strong>Remake</strong></td><td>fresh</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ov171</td><td><code>0x778</code></td><td>16</td><td><strong>Remake</strong></td><td>fresh</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Currency canon</h2>
  <p>
    Game 1's official Konami currency name is <strong>Ritch</strong> (not
    "Mira"). Evidence: 66 corpus hits, including the standalone slot-form
    <code>リッチ → Ritch</code>. The 6 currency tier strings:
    <code>1リッチ → 1 R</code>, <code>10リッチ → 10 R</code>, …,
    <code>100000リッチ → 100000 Ritch</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Slot budgets vs canonical EN length</h2>
  <p>
    20 slots cannot fit the full Konami EN canon within their fixed slot
    stride. The constraint is structural: each slot is allocated a
    pre-computed byte width in the pool, and any string longer than that
    byte width would overrun into the next slot. A pool relocator that
    rewrote the pointer table and grew the pool would relax these
    per-slot byte budgets; that is not currently in scope. The table below
    records the gap between the byte-budget-fitting EN and the longer
    canonical Konami EN so the cost is visible. The currency tiers and
    "Change wallpaper" / "Change flooring" are the highest-information-loss
    cases; <code>Hat off</code>, <code>No spex</code>, and
    <code>Release</code> are close-enough-to-canon semantically.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Slot</th><th>Fits in slot</th><th>Full Konami canon</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {CO_LIST.map((r) => (
        <tr>
          <td><code>{r.slot}</code></td>
          <td><code>{r.current}</code></td>
          <td><strong>{r.fullCanon}</strong></td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Slot accounting</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Count</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>canon-G1 (full canon, fits)</td><td>5 (Equip ×4, Unequip ×1, 100000 Ritch)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>canon-mine (canon overflow, shortened)</td><td>20</td></tr>
      <tr><td>canon (shop name, fits)</td><td>1 (Berry's at ov96 0x600)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>corpus (G1 dialog inference)</td><td>3 (Save / Mystery / Rumors)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>fresh</td><td>23</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Total slots filled</strong></td><td><strong>52</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Total unique strings</strong></td><td><strong>49</strong> (Equip ×3 + Remake ×3 = 6 duplicate slots)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Source</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>notes/arm9_overlay_translations_final.md</code> — full string list + budgets</li>
    <li><code>src/translator/_patch_arm9_overlays.py</code> — patcher</li>
    <li><code>notes/arm9_passthrough_test_RESULTS.md</code> — BLZ-skip trick validation</li>
    <li><code>arm9_full_plaintext.bin</code> — decompressed binary used for patching</li>
  </ul>
</FieldGuideLayout>
